<title>BRAZIL: Soybean trucks in 40-km queue at Brazil Paranagua.</title>
<headline>Soybean trucks in 40-km queue at Brazil Paranagua.</headline>
<dateline>SAO PAULO 1997-03-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>Trucks loaded with soybeans formed a 40 kilometer (24 mile) traffic jam on motorways leading to the key Brazilian port of Paranagua as faster-than-expected harvesting caused a rush to deliver, the port authority said.</p>
<p>&quot;There was a 40 kilometer queue this morning but it's already easing,&quot; a spokeswoman said.</p>
<p>Globo Television reported that 5,000 trucks were queued up to enter the port but the spokeswoman put the figure closer to 2,000.</p>
<p>&quot;At the weekend, 4,000 trucks arrived which was well above expectations,&quot; she said. &quot;Firms are calling in trucks to deliver in their warehouses (at the port).&quot;</p>
<p>The port has a parking capacity for 1,500 trucks and warehousing capacity for 900,000 tonnes of grains. The port's export terminal is operating 24 hours a day. &quot;The silos are practically full,&quot; the spokeswoman said.</p>
<p>((--Joelle Diderich, Sao Paulo newsroom +55 11 248 5417))</p>
